{"key":"inputs","status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"id\": \"packet.json\", \"pointer\": \"/inputs\", \"view\": \"value\", \"value\": [{\"id\": \"H001\", \"kind\": \"hypothesis\", \"statement\": \"p is prime, K has characteristic p, and λ is an integer with 0≤λ<p.\", \"definitions\": [], \"binders\": [], \"objects\": [\"p\", \"K\", \"λ\"]}, {\"id\": \"N001\", \"kind\": \"internal\", \"statement\": \"n=λ+1>0 and |I|=n. The vectors (e_i)_(i∈I) form a complex basis of V. For every integer t≥0, h y^t v=(λ−2t)y^t v, and for t≥1, x y^t v=t(λ−t+1)y^(t−1)v. Moreover y^(λ+1)v=0 and all higher powers on v vanish. Their action matrices are x e_0=0, x e_i=(λ−i+1)e_(i−1) for 1≤i≤λ; y e_i=(i+1)e_(i+1) for 0≤i<λ, y e_λ=0; h e_i=(λ−2i)e_i for i∈I. For every integer r≥0 and i∈I, X(r)e_i=binom(λ−i+r,r)e_(i−r) if r≤i and zero otherwise; Y(r)e_i=binom(i+r,r)e_(i+r) if i+r≤λ and zero otherwise. Each displayed nonzero coefficient is an integer. Here binom(a,r)=a!/(r!(a−r)!) for integers 0≤r≤a; these are the usual integer binomial coefficients.\", \"definitions\": [\"D001\", \"D002\", \"D003\", \"D004\", \"D006\"], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"t\", \"type\": \"nonnegative integer\"}, {\"symbol\": \"r\", \"type\": \"nonnegative integer\"}, {\"symbol\": \"i\", \"type\": \"element of I\"}, {\"symbol\": \"a\", \"type\": \"integer at least r\"}], \"objects\": [\"n\", \"λ\", \"I\", \"e\", \"V\", \"h\", \"x\", \"y\", \"v\", \"X\", \"Y\", \"t\", \"r\", \"i\", \"a\"]}, {\"id\": \"N002\", \"kind\": \"internal\", \"statement\": \"R is the subring ℤ_(p) of ℚ⊂ℂ. The map ι_M:R⊗_ℤ M→V is injective. With M_R identified with its image it is a free R-module with an R-basis of n elements and its complex span is V. Each X(r),Y(r), r≥0, preserves M and M_R. The operators x,y,h preserve M; ℤx+ℤy+ℤh is closed under the Lie bracket. For t∈ℤx+ℤy+ℤh, m∈M and c,d∈K, the rule (t⊗c)(m⊗d)=tm⊗(cd) is well-defined on (ℤx+ℤy+ℤh)⊗_ℤ K and V_K=M⊗_ℤ K and defines a K-linear Lie action. For every integer 0≤r≤λ, p∤r!, so r! is a unit in R. For all i,j∈I, binom(λ,j) is a unit in R and Π=E_λλ, X(λ−i)Π=E_iλ, ΠY(λ−j)=binom(λ,j)E_λj, and E_ij=binom(λ,j)^(-1)X(λ−i)ΠY(λ−j). Consequently all E_ij preserve M_R.\", \"definitions\": [\"D001\", \"D002\", \"D003\", \"D004\", \"D007\", \"D008\", \"D009\", \"D010\", \"D011\", \"D014\"], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"r\", \"type\": \"nonnegative integer\"}, {\"symbol\": \"i\", \"type\": \"element of I\"}, {\"symbol\": \"j\", \"type\": \"element of I\"}, {\"symbol\": \"t\", \"type\": \"element of ℤx+ℤy+ℤh\"}, {\"symbol\": \"m\", \"type\": \"element of M\"}, {\"symbol\": \"c\", \"type\": \"element of K\"}, {\"symbol\": \"d\", \"type\": \"element of K\"}], \"objects\": [\"R\", \"p\", \"ι_M\", \"M\", \"M_R\", \"V\", \"n\", \"X\", \"Y\", \"x\", \"y\", \"h\", \"λ\", \"Π\", \"E\", \"I\", \"r\", \"i\", \"j\", \"t\", \"m\", \"c\", \"d\", \"K\", \"V_K\"]}, {\"id\": \"M001\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"statement\": \"For every division ring F, additive commutative group A with an F-module structure, finite index type Γ, and finite-dimensional F-vector space A, if f:Γ→A is F-linearly independent and |Γ|=finrank_F(A), then span_F(range f)=A.\", \"definitions\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"F\", \"type\": \"division ring\"}, {\"symbol\": \"A\", \"type\": \"additive commutative group and F-module\"}, {\"symbol\": \"Γ\", \"type\": \"finite index type\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"family Γ→A\"}], \"objects\": [\"F\", \"A\", \"Γ\", \"f\"], \"source_contract\": {\"hypotheses\": [\"F is a division ring, and A is an additive commutative group with an F-module structure.\", \"Γ is finite and A is finite-dimensional over F.\", \"The family f is F-linearly independent.\", \"|Γ|=finrank_F(A).\"], \"declaration\": \"LinearIndependent.span_eq_top_of_card_eq_finrank'\", \"signature\": \"@LinearIndependent.span_eq_top_of_card_eq_finrank' : ∀ {K : Type u} {V : Type v} [inst : DivisionRing.{u} K] [inst_1 : AddCommGroup.{v} V]\\n  [inst_2 : _root_.Module.{u, v} K V] {ι : Type u_1} [inst_3 : Fintype.{u_1} ι] [FiniteDimensional.{u, v} K V]\\n  {b : ι → V},\\n  LinearIndependent.{u_1, u, v} K b →\\n    Eq.{1} (Fintype.card.{u_1} ι) (Module.finrank.{u, v} K V) →\\n      Eq.{v + 1} (Submodule.span.{u, v} K (Set.range.{v, u_1 + 1} b)) Top.top.{v}\"}}, {\"id\": \"M002\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"statement\": \"For every commutative semiring R₀, additive commutative monoid A with an R₀-module structure, index type Γ, semiring S₀ with an R₀-algebra structure, R₀-basis B:Γ→A, and i∈Γ, the canonical S₀-basis B.baseChange(S₀) of S₀⊗_(R₀)A has ith vector 1⊗B_i. The canonical basis is obtained by tensoring the singleton S₀-basis of S₀ with B and reindexing Γ.\", \"definitions\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"R₀\", \"type\": \"commutative semiring\"}, {\"symbol\": \"A\", \"type\": \"additive commutative monoid and R₀-module\"}, {\"symbol\": \"Γ\", \"type\": \"index type\"}, {\"symbol\": \"S₀\", \"type\": \"semiring and R₀-algebra\"}, {\"symbol\": \"B\", \"type\": \"R₀-basis of A indexed by Γ\"}, {\"symbol\": \"i\", \"type\": \"element of Γ\"}], \"objects\": [\"R₀\", \"A\", \"Γ\", \"S₀\", \"B\", \"i\"], \"source_contract\": {\"hypotheses\": [\"R₀ is a commutative semiring and A is an additive commutative monoid and R₀-module.\", \"S₀ is a semiring with an R₀-algebra structure.\", \"B is an R₀-basis of A indexed by Γ and i∈Γ.\"], \"declaration\": \"Module.Basis.baseChange_apply\", \"signature\": \"@Module.Basis.baseChange_apply : ∀ {R : Type u_1} {M : Type u_3} {ι : Type u_5} [inst : CommSemiring.{u_1} R] [inst_1 : AddCommMonoid.{u_3} M]\\n  [inst_2 : _root_.Module.{u_1, u_3} R M] (S : Type u_7) [inst_3 : Semiring.{u_7} S] [inst_4 : Algebra.{u_1, u_7} R S]\\n  (b : Module.Basis.{u_5, u_1, u_3} ι R M) (i : ι),\\n  Eq.{max (u_3 + 1) (u_7 + 1)} ((Module.Basis.baseChange.{u_1, u_3, u_5, u_7} S b) i)\\n    (TensorProduct.tmul.{u_1, u_7, u_3} R 1 (b i))\"}}, {\"id\": \"M003\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"statement\": \"For every semiring R₀ satisfying the strong rank condition, additive commutative monoid A with an R₀-module structure, finite index type Γ and R₀-basis B indexed by Γ, finrank_(R₀)(A)=|Γ|. The strong rank condition means that an injective linear map R₀^u→R₀^w between finite free modules forces u≤w.\", \"definitions\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"R₀\", \"type\": \"semiring satisfying the strong rank condition\"}, {\"symbol\": \"A\", \"type\": \"additive commutative monoid and R₀-module\"}, {\"symbol\": \"Γ\", \"type\": \"finite index type\"}, {\"symbol\": \"B\", \"type\": \"R₀-basis of A indexed by Γ\"}, {\"symbol\": \"u\", \"type\": \"natural number\"}, {\"symbol\": \"w\", \"type\": \"natural number\"}], \"objects\": [\"R₀\", \"A\", \"Γ\", \"B\", \"u\", \"w\"], \"source_contract\": {\"hypotheses\": [\"R₀ is a semiring satisfying the strong rank condition.\", \"A is an additive commutative monoid with an R₀-module structure.\", \"Γ is finite and B is an R₀-basis of A indexed by Γ.\"], \"declaration\": \"Module.finrank_eq_card_basis\", \"signature\": \"@Module.finrank_eq_card_basis : ∀ {R : Type u} {M : Type v} [inst : Semiring.{u} R] [inst_1 : AddCommMonoid.{v} M] [inst_2 : _root_.Module.{u, v} R M]\\n  [StrongRankCondition.{u} R] {ι : Type w} [inst_4 : Fintype.{w} ι] (h : Module.Basis.{w, u, v} ι R M),\\n  Eq.{1} (Module.finrank.{u, v} R M) (Fintype.card.{w} ι)\"}}, {\"id\": \"M004\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"statement\": \"For every semiring R₀ satisfying the strong rank condition, finite index type Γ, and family A_i of additive commutative monoids with R₀-module structures, if each A_i is free and finitely generated over R₀, then finrank_(R₀)(∏_(i∈Γ) A_i)=Σ_(i∈Γ) finrank_(R₀)(A_i).\", \"definitions\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"R₀\", \"type\": \"semiring satisfying the strong rank condition\"}, {\"symbol\": \"Γ\", \"type\": \"finite index type\"}, {\"symbol\": \"A_i\", \"type\": \"family of additive commutative monoids and R₀-modules indexed by Γ\"}, {\"symbol\": \"i\", \"type\": \"element of Γ\"}], \"objects\": [\"R₀\", \"Γ\", \"A_i\", \"i\"], \"source_contract\": {\"hypotheses\": [\"R₀ is a semiring satisfying the strong rank condition.\", \"Γ is finite.\", \"Every A_i is an …3328 tokens truncated…ts\": [\"k\", \"q\", \"p\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"u_j\", \"type\": \"family of units of R indexed by 1,...,k\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S007\", \"end\": \"S007\", \"kind\": \"witness\", \"declaration\": \"Choose the unit family u_j supplied by F006, so q_j=p^(ν_j)u_j.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [\"F006\"], \"objects\": [\"k\", \"q\", \"p\", \"R\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"ν\", \"type\": \"integer\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S007\", \"end\": \"S007\", \"kind\": \"definition\", \"declaration\": \"Set ν=min_{1≤j≤k}ν_j.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"k\", \"ν_j\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"j₀\", \"type\": \"integer with 1≤j₀≤k\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S007\", \"end\": \"S007\", \"kind\": \"witness\", \"declaration\": \"Choose an index j₀ attaining the minimum ν of the nonempty finite family ν_j; a minimum of finitely many integers is one of them.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [\"F006\"], \"objects\": [\"ν\", \"ν_j\", \"k\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"b\", \"type\": \"complex number\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S007\", \"end\": \"S007\", \"kind\": \"definition\", \"declaration\": \"Define b=p^ν a, interpreting the integer power in ℚ⊂ℂ.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"p\", \"ν\", \"a\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"q₀\", \"type\": \"rational number\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S004\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix an arbitrary rational q₀ for testing the tensor formulas.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": []}, {\"symbol\": \"r_j\", \"type\": \"family of elements of R indexed by 1,...,k\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S002\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"For the spanning calculation take arbitrary R-coefficients r_j.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"R\", \"k\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"s₀\", \"type\": \"nonnegative integer\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S002\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix the number s₀ of summands in an arbitrary finite tensor presentation.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": []}, {\"symbol\": \"Q\", \"type\": \"family of rationals indexed by 1,...,s₀\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S002\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix any rational list Q for the finite tensor presentation.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"s₀\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"C\", \"type\": \"family of elements of J indexed by 1,...,s₀\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S002\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix any list C in J for the finite tensor presentation.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"s₀\", \"J\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"z₀\", \"type\": \"element of U\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S002\", \"kind\": \"definition\", \"declaration\": \"Set z₀=Σ_(l=1)^(s₀)Q_l⊗C_l.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"U\", \"s₀\", \"Q\", \"C\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"D\", \"type\": \"nonzero integer\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S002\", \"kind\": \"definition\", \"declaration\": \"Set D equal to the product of the positive rational denominators of the Q_l, with empty product one.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"Q\", \"s₀\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"H\", \"type\": \"integer family indexed by 1,...,s₀\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S002\", \"kind\": \"definition\", \"declaration\": \"Set H_l=D Q_l; the product of denominators makes these integers.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"D\", \"Q\", \"s₀\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"i\", \"type\": \"element of I\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S001\", \"end\": \"S001\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix an arbitrary coordinate index i∈I.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"I\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"j₁\", \"type\": \"element of I\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S001\", \"end\": \"S001\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix another arbitrary coordinate index j₁∈I.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"I\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"c₁\", \"type\": \"element of J_(j₁)\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S001\", \"end\": \"S001\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix an arbitrary c₁∈J_(j₁) for the inclusion argument.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"J_i\", \"j₁\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"z\", \"type\": \"element of M_R\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S004\", \"end\": \"S004\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix an arbitrary z∈M_R for evaluating Θ on pure tensors.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"M_R\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"c\", \"type\": \"element of J\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S004\", \"end\": \"S004\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix an arbitrary c∈J for the coordinate inclusion in Ψ.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"J\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"c_i\", \"type\": \"family I→J\", \"scope\": \"R003/argument\", \"start\": \"S004\", \"end\": \"S004\", \"kind\": \"definition\", \"declaration\": \"Write c_i for the unique e-coordinates of z supplied by F001, so z=Σ_(i∈I)c_i e_i.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [\"F001\"], \"objects\": [\"z\", \"e\", \"I\", \"J\"]}], \"argument\": [{\"id\": \"S001\", \"text\": \"Use the embedding from N002 throughout to regard M_R as a submodule of V. By N001 its vectors have unique coordinates in e. Since E_ii preserves M_R by N002, the ith coordinate projection maps M_R onto J_i e_i. For arbitrary i,j₁∈I and c₁∈J_(j₁), E(i,j₁)(c₁ e_(j₁))=c₁ e_i∈M_R, giving J_(j₁)⊂J_i; the reverse inclusion follows by exchanging the two coordinate indices. Thus all J_i equal J. Choose the R-basis m of M_R supplied by N002. The finitely many ith coordinates of m generate J_i, since each vector of M_R is a finite R-linear combination of m, and conversely these coordinates lie in J_i by E_ii-stability. Hence J is finitely generated. It is nonzero: temporarily assume L1: J={0}. Then every coordinate projection of M_R would vanish, so M_R would be zero, contradicting its complex span V and the nonzero basis vector e_0 in N001. This contradiction discharges L1 and proves J≠{0}. Finally the maps M_R→J^I taking coordinates, and (c_i)↦Σ c_i e_i, are mutually inverse R-linear maps. The latter sum is in M_R since each c_i e_i is; independence in N001 gives uniqueness. This proves the direct sum decomposition without any rank assumption on a submodule of ℂ.\", \"uses\": [\"H001\", \"N001\", \"N002\"], \"uses_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"ι_M\", \"M_R\", \"V\", \"e\", \"E\", \"I\", \"J_i\", \"J\", \"R\", \"m\", \"i\", \"j₁\", \"c₁\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F001\", \"statement\": \"For every i′∈I, J_i′=J. The R-module J is nonzero and finitely generated. The coordinate map M_R→J^I and its inverse, sending a coefficient family to its finite expansion in e, are R-linear inverses. In particular there is a finite nonempty list of nonzero R-generators of J.\", \"objects\": [\"J_i\", \"J\", \"I\", \"M_R\", \"R\", \"e\", \"i′\"], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"i′\", \"type\": \"element of I\"}], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [\"L1\"], \"discharges\": [\"L1\"]}, {\"id\": \"S002\", \"text\": \"Define U and μ as declared, and choose a nonempty finite list g of nonzero R-generators of J from F001; let k≥1 be its length, and write it g_1,...,g_k. For any rational q₀ and any finite R-linear combination Σ_(j=1)^k r_j g_j, tensor bilinearity gives q₀⊗Σ r_j g_j=Σ(q₀r_j)(1⊗g_j). Consequently the finite set {1⊗g_j:1≤j≤k} spans U over ℚ. Apply M008 with F=ℚ and A=U to this finite set; since the span is all of U, U is finite-dimensional. To check injectivity of μ, let s₀≥0 be arbitrary, take arbitrary families Q_1,...,Q_(s₀)∈ℚ and C_1,...,C_(s₀)∈J, and set z₀=Σ_(l=1)^(s₀)Q_l⊗C_l. Every element of the algebraic tensor product has such a finite-sum presentation. Define D to be the product of the positive denominators of the Q_l (the empty product is 1), and put H_l=D Q_l∈ℤ. Then D is a nonzero integer and tensor balancing over R, which contains ℤ, gives z₀=(1/D)⊗Σ H_l C_l. Temporarily assume L2: μ(z₀)=0. Its image is (1/D)Σ H_l C_l, so Σ H_l C_l=0 in ℂ, hence z₀=0 in U. Discharge L2. Since the finite presentation was arbitrary, the kernel of μ is zero, which proves injectivity by applying this to the difference of any two tensors with equal images. Its image is exactly span_ℚ J, since it consists of finite rational combinations of elements of J and contains them all.\", \"uses\": [\"M008\"], \"uses_local\": [\"F001\"], \"objects\": [\"U\", \"μ\", \"J\", \"R\", \"g\", \"k\", \"q₀\", \"r_j\", \"s₀\", \"Q\", \"C\", \"z₀\", \"D\", \"H\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F002\", \"statement\": \"U=ℚ⊗_R J is finite-dimensional. The map μ:U→ℂ is injective, satisfies μ(q′⊗c′)=q′·c′ for all q′∈ℚ and c′∈J, and has image span_ℚ J.\", \"objects\": [\"U\", \"μ\", \"J\", \"R\", \"q′\", \"c′\"], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"q′\", \"type\": \"rational number\"}, {\"symbol\": \"c′\", \"type\": \"element of J\"}], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [\"L2\"], \"discharges\": [\"L2\"]}, {\"id\": \"S003\", \"text\": \"Define T=ℚ⊗_R M_R. The inclusion R⊂ℚ gives a commutative R-algebra structure on ℚ. Apply M002 to the R-basis m: the n vectors 1⊗m_i form a ℚ-basis of T. M010 applies to the nontrivial commutative ring ℚ. M003 with this basis gives dim_ℚ T=n, since |I|=n by N001.\", \"uses\": [\"M002\", \"M003\", \"M010\", \"N001\"], \"uses_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"T\", \"R\", \"M_R\", \"m\", \"I\", \"n\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F003\", \"statement\": \"dim_ℚ T=n, and the vectors 1⊗m_i indexed by I form a ℚ-basis of T.\", \"binders\": [], \"objects\": [\"T\", \"m\", \"I\", \"n\"], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}, {\"id\": \"S004\", \"text\": \"Define Θ:T→U^I by Θ(q₀⊗Σ c_i e_i)=(q₀⊗c_i)_i, and define Ψ in the reverse direction by summing the maps q₀⊗c↦q₀⊗(c e_i) on coordinates. The coordinate maps in F001 are R-linear, so these balanced maps extend to ℚ-linear maps of the indicated tensor products. The composites ΘΨ and ΨΘ are identities: on an elementary tensor in a single coordinate the first is that same coordinate tensor, and on q₀⊗z the second is q₀⊗Σ c_i e_i=q₀⊗z. Additivity and generation by pure tensors prove the identities everywhere. Therefore Θ is a linear equivalence. Set r_J=dim_ℚ U. By F002, U is finitely generated over ℚ, and M009 says it is free. With M010 supplying the strong rank condition for ℚ, apply M004 to the finite constant family U indexed by I; it gives dim_ℚ(U^I)=Σ_(i∈I)r_J=n r_J. Apply M005 to Θ and combine with F003 to obtain n=n r_J. Since n>0 by N001, integer arithmetic yields r_J=1.\", \"uses\": [\"M004\", \"M005\", \"M009\", \"M010\", \"N001\"], \"uses_local\": [\"F001\", \"F002\", \"F003\"], \"objects\": [\"Θ\", \"Ψ\", \"T\", \"U\", \"r_J\", \"n\", \"I\", \"J\", \"e\", \"M_R\", \"R\", \"q₀\", \"z\", \"c\", \"c_i\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F004\", \"statement\": \"dim_ℚ U=r_J=1.\", \"binders\": [], \"objects\": [\"U\", \"r_J\"], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}, {\"id\": \"S005\", \"text\": \"Choose a nonzero a∈J from F001. Then 1⊗a is nonzero in U, since μ(1⊗a)=a≠0. The singleton family consisting of 1⊗a is linearly independent: multiplying a putative scalar relation by μ gives that scalar times a zero in ℂ. Apply M001 over ℚ to this singleton and U, whose finite dimension is one by F002 and F004. It spans U. Consequently every 1⊗g_j is q_j(1⊗a) for some rational q_j; apply μ to get g_j=q_j a. As every g_j≠0, each q_j≠0. This proves existence of the full finite rational family needed next.\", \"uses\": [\"M001\"], \"uses_local\": [\"F001\", \"F002\", \"F004\"], \"objects\": [\"a\", \"J\", \"U\", \"μ\", \"g\", \"k\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F005\", \"statement\": \"There exists a family q′:{1,...,k}→ℚ\\\\{0} such that g(j′)=q′(j′)a for every integer j′ with 1≤j′≤k.\", \"objects\": [\"g\", \"k\", \"a\", \"q′\", \"j′\"], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"q′\", \"type\": \"family of nonzero rational numbers indexed by 1,...,k\"}, {\"symbol\": \"j′\", \"type\": \"integer with 1≤j′≤k\"}], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}, {\"id\": \"S006\", \"text\": \"Choose q from F005, and fix 1≤j≤k. Write q_j=A/B with its nonzero integer numerator A and positive integer denominator B. By H001, p≥2, so p≠1. Apply M007 first to |A|≠0, then to B≠0, with ℓ=p. Choose d_N,u_N,d_D,u_D as in those applications, so |A|=p^d_N u_N, B=p^d_D u_D and neither u_N nor u_D is divisible by p. They are nonzero because A,B are. Hence q_j=p^(d_N−d_D)((A/|A|)u_N/u_D). Since A/|A| is either 1 or −1, the rational (A/|A|)u_N/u_D and its inverse belong to R by the defining denominator condition, so this factor is a unit. This proves, for every j in the finite list, the claimed decomposition into an integral power of p and a unit of R, and therefore the existence of simultaneous finite families in F006.\", \"uses\": [\"M007\", \"H001\"], \"uses_local\": [\"F005\"], \"objects\": [\"q\", \"g\", \"a\", \"j\", \"k\", \"A\", \"B\", \"p\", \"R\", \"d_N\", \"u_N\", \"d_D\", \"u_D\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F006\", \"statement\": \"There exist families ν′:{1,...,k}→ℤ and u′:{1,...,k}→Rˣ such that q(j′)=p^(ν′(j′))u′(j′) for every integer j′ with 1≤j′≤k. Here Rˣ means the set of elements of R possessing a multiplicative inverse in R.\", \"objects\": [\"q\", \"p\", \"R\", \"k\", \"ν′\", \"u′\", \"j′\"], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"ν′\", \"type\": \"family of integers indexed by 1,...,k\"}, {\"symbol\": \"u′\", \"type\": \"family of units of R indexed by 1,...,k\"}, {\"symbol\": \"j′\", \"type\": \"integer with 1≤j′≤k\"}], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}, {\"id\": \"S007\", \"text\": \"Choose ν_j,u_j from F006 and let ν be the minimum of the finite nonempty integer list ν_1,...,ν_k; choose j₀ attaining it. Such a minimum exists by repeatedly taking the smaller of two integers. Put b=p^ν a. This is nonzero, since p≠0 and a≠0. For each j, g_j=p^(ν_j−ν)u_j b lies in Rb because ν_j−ν≥0. Conversely g_(j₀)=u_(j₀)b, so b=u_(j₀)^(-1)g_(j₀) belongs to J. Thus the R-module generated by the g_j is exactly Rb, and J=Rb. Under F001, every vector of M_R is a sum Σ r_i b e_i, while any relation Σ r_i b e_i=0 has all r_i b=0 by the complex independence of e in N001, and hence all r_i=0. Thus (b e_i) is an R-basis. The same spanning and independence argument works for any nonzero b with J=Rb. Together with F001, F002 and F004 this proves the exported conclusion.\", \"uses\": [\"H001\", \"N001\"], \"uses_local\": [\"F001\", \"F002\", \"F004\", \"F005\", \"F006\"], \"objects\": [\"ν_j\", \"u_j\", \"ν\", \"j₀\", \"b\", \"p\", \"a\", \"g\", \"q\", \"k\", \"R\", \"J\", \"M_R\", \"e\", \"I\", \"μ\", \"U\", \"r_J\"], \"produces\": [], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}], \"applications\": [{\"input\": \"M008\", \"at\": \"S002\", \"bindings\": \"F=ℚ, A=U=ℚ⊗_R J and source finite set B={1⊗g_j:1≤j≤k}.\", \"hypothesis_checks\": [\"ℚ is a field; the tensor extension U is a ℚ-vector space.\", \"The chosen generating family is finite by F001; S002 proves its tensor images span U, so the finite-dimensional span is U itself.\"]}, {\"input\": \"M002\", \"at\": \"S003\", \"bindings\": \"R₀=R, S₀=ℚ, A=M_R, Γ=I and source basis B=m, applied for all i∈I.\", \"hypothesis_checks\": [\"R is a commutative subring of ℚ by N002 and M_R is an R-module.\", \"The inclusion R⊂ℚ supplies the R-algebra structure on ℚ.\", \"m was chosen from the R-basis assertion of N002 and reindexed using |I|=n from N001.\"]}, {\"input\": \"M003\", \"at\": \"S003\", \"bindings\": \"R₀=ℚ, A=T, Γ=I, source B=(1⊗m_i)_(i∈I).\", \"hypothesis_checks\": [\"ℚ is a field and M010 in S003 supplies its strong rank condition.\", \"T is a ℚ-vector space by its tensor construction.\", \"I is finite by N001; the preceding M002 application in S003 supplies this basis and N001 gives |I|=n.\"]}, {\"input\": \"M010\", \"at\": \"S003\", \"id\": \"A001\", \"bindings\": \"R₀=ℚ.\", \"hypothesis_checks\": [\"ℚ is a commutative ring.\", \"0≠1 in ℚ.\"]}, {\"input\": \"M010\", \"at\": \"S004\", \"id\": \"A002\", \"bindings\": \"R₀=ℚ.\", \"hypothesis_checks\": [\"ℚ is a commutative ring.\", \"0≠1 in ℚ.\"]}, {\"input\": \"M009\", \"at\": \"S004\", \"bindings\": \"F=ℚ, A=U.\", \"hypothesis_checks\": [\"ℚ is a field.\", \"U is a ℚ-vector space by its tensor definition.\"]}, {\"input\": \"M004\", \"at\": \"S004\", \"bindings\": \"R₀=ℚ, Γ=I, and A_i=U for every i∈I.\", \"hypothesis_checks\": [\"M010 in S004 supplies the strong rank condition for the field ℚ.\", \"N001 says I has the finite cardinality n.\", \"U is a ℚ-vector space by its tensor construction.\", \"The preceding M009 application in S004 gives freeness of U.\", \"F002 and the displayed finite tensor spanning family in S002 give finite generation of U over ℚ.\"]}, {\"input\": \"M005\", \"at\": \"S004\", \"bindings\": \"R₀=ℚ, A=T, B=U^I and f=Θ.\", \"hypothesis_checks\": [\"T and U^I are ℚ-vector spaces.\", \"S004 constructs Θ and Ψ from the tensor balanced maps and verifies that their two composites are the identity, so Θ is a ℚ-linear equivalence.\"]}, {\"input\": \"M001\", \"at\": \"S005\", \"bindings\": \"F=ℚ, A=U, Γ=a singleton, and f its constant value 1⊗a.\", \"hypothesis_checks\": [\"ℚ is a field and U a ℚ-vector space.\", \"The singleton is finite; F002 establishes finite dimensionality of U.\", \"If c(1⊗a)=0, applying μ gives ca=0, hence c=0 because a≠0; S005 displays this independence proof.\", \"The singleton has cardinality one, equal to dim_ℚ U by F004.\"]}, {\"input\": \"M007\", \"at\": \"S006\", \"id\": \"A003\", \"bindings\": \"Source u=|A| and ℓ=p, for each nonzero rational q_j=A/B.\", \"hypothesis_checks\": [\"A≠0 because q_j≠0 in F005; hence its natural absolute value is nonzero.\", \"p≥2 by H001, so p≠1.\"]}, {\"input\": \"M007\", \"at\": \"S006\", \"id\": \"A004\", \"bindings\": \"Source u=B, the positive denominator of q_j, and ℓ=p.\", \"hypothesis_checks\": [\"The rational denominator B is positive, so B≠0.\", \"p≥2 by H001, so p≠1.\"]}]}, \"has_more\": false, \"next_offset\": null}"}
