{id:"BG001",at:"/inference/argument/1/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M901",fact:"For R-modules A,B over a commutative semiring, every element of A⊗_R B is a finite sum of pure tensors. Tensor identities preserved by addition can be checked on zero and pure tensors. Used for U=ℚ⊗_R J and T=ℚ⊗_R M_R in S002 and S004.",grounds:[...base,{location:"/inference/locals/5/declaration",reason:"T is the other algebraic tensor product used in S004."}],justification:"The probed induction_on has exactly zero, pure-tensor, and addition cases. A finite-sum representation follows by the empty sum, singleton, and concatenation cases. Equality of additive maps follows by the same induction. All factors are additive commutative groups with R-module structures; R is a commutative subring. This supplies only routine tensor generation, not injectivity, a decomposition or a rank computation."},
{id:"BG002",at:"/inference/argument/1/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M902",fact:"q⊗(c+d)=q⊗c+q⊗d in ℚ⊗_R J, and likewise with J replaced by M_R; consequently tensoring respects the displayed finite sums in S002 and S004.",grounds:base,justification:"Apply the checked tmul_add with base ring R and factors ℚ,J (or M_R). All additive commutative monoid and module premises follow from the stated subring and module structures. Iteration handles a finite sum; its empty case follows by adding zero and cancellation in the additive group."},
{id:"BG003",at:"/inference/argument/1/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M903",fact:"(q+q′)⊗c=q⊗c+q′⊗c for q,q′∈ℚ and c∈J, also with M_R as second factor.",grounds:base,justification:"The actual add_tmul signature requires only a commutative semiring and two additive commutative monoids with modules. R,ℚ,J and M_R meet these conditions. This validates additivity of the balanced formulas, not any asserted core conclusion."},
{id:"BG004",at:"/inference/argument/1/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M904",fact:"For r∈R, q∈ℚ and c∈J, (qr)⊗c=q⊗(rc), likewise for c∈M_R. In particular Q_l⊗C_l=(1/D)⊗(H_l C_l) whenever D≠0 and H_l=D Q_l∈ℤ⊂R, as in S002.",grounds:[...base,{location:"/inference/argument/1/text",reason:"The paragraph constructs the nonzero integer D and integers H_l; it explicitly uses only these integer scalars for denominator clearing."}],justification:"Use smul_tmul with R′=R; both factors have their usual R-module actions, so CompatibleSMul is precisely base-ring balancing (the read source derives it from the identity scalar towers). Multiplication in ℚ commutes, and (1/D)H_l=Q_l. No claim that 1/D belongs to R is needed. The remaining kernel argument is displayed in S002."},
{id:"BG005",at:"/inference/argument/1/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M906",fact:"For s,q∈ℚ and c∈J, s·(q⊗c)=(sq)⊗c in ℚ⊗_R J; the same holds with M_R as second factor.",grounds:base,justification:"Apply smul_tmul′ with base R and external scalar monoid ℚ acting on the first factor ℚ. R and ℚ scalar actions commute because they are multiplications in the commutative field ℚ. Both tensor factors have the required R-module structures. Together with balancing this verifies q⊗r g=(qr)(1⊗g) in the author's spanning calculation."},
{id:"BG006",at:"/inference/argument/1/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M905",fact:"The balanced multiplication (q,c)↦qc, q∈ℚ,c∈J, extends to a ℚ-linear map μ:ℚ⊗_R J→ℂ with μ(q⊗c)=qc.",grounds:[...base,{location:"/inference/locals/2/declaration",reason:"This is exactly the declared μ and its proposed pure-tensor formula."}],justification:"Instantiate lift_tmul with base R, scalar algebra A=ℚ, first module M=ℚ, second module N=J and target P=ℂ. Multiplication is ℚ-linear in q and R-linear in c by distributivity and associativity. The R-algebra ℚ is given by the inclusion; the R→ℚ→ℚ and R→ℚ→ℂ scalar towers hold for these same inclusions. The checked lift has a ℚ-linear-map type and the exact pure-tensor value. This routine universal property grants no injectivity; S002 separately proves injectivity."},
{id:"BG007",at:"/inference/argument/3/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M905",fact:"The maps (q,z)↦(q⊗c_i(z))_i and (q,c)↦q⊗(c e_i) extend to ℚ-linear maps T→U^I and U→T, respectively; their finite coordinate sum is the declared Ψ.",grounds:[...base,{location:"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement",reason:"F001 proves that z↦c_i(z) and c↦c e_i are R-linear, with values in J and M_R."},{location:"/inference/argument/3/text",reason:"S004 specifies the two balanced formulas and the finite sum over I."},{location:"/inputs/1/statement",reason:"N001 gives finiteness of I."}],justification:"Use the checked lift_tmul with scalar algebra ℚ, first factor ℚ, second factor M_R and target U^I for Θ; for the coordinate maps defining Ψ use second factor J and target T. The curried formulas are ℚ-linear in the first entry and R-linear in the second by the linear coordinate maps and tensor bilinearity checked above. All scalar towers are inherited from R⊂ℚ and scalar extension. This admits only the basic extension operation. The two inverse identities, including their coordinate calculation, remain the author's displayed proof in S004."}
